    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking at some testing data for our new high-performance braking assembly. We're seeing significant thermal soak issues with the semi-metallic pads during repeated high-speed deceleration cycles, whereas the ceramic composite pads seem to maintain a much more stable friction coefficient. Can you explain the primary differences in heat dissipation mechanisms between these two material types and how that affects the thermal management of the rotor?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Semi-metallic brake pads generate more friction, leading to higher temperatures and thus better heat resistance, but also dissipate heat more slowly. Ceramic composite pads offer lower coefficient of friction and dissipate heat quickly, keeping the rotor temperature more stable over time. The differences in thermal conduction rates between the materials significantly impact the thermal management of the rotor, causing semi-metallic pads to affect rotor temperatures while the ceramic composite pads create a more consistent friction environment for repeated use.",
      "source": "target",
