    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but I'm trying to dig deeper into the failure modes. In some of our high-stress test runs, the semi-metallic pads seem to \"act up\" unexpectedly—we're seeing sudden, erratic shifts in brake pedal feel after a certain thermal threshold is hit. It almost feels like there's an unpredictable element or an internal interference causing the inconsistency. Is there a specific metallurgical or structural reason why the material properties would behave so erratically once they reach those peak temps?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "At high temperatures, semi-metallic brake pads can experience the breakdown of the material components and fracturing of the pad structure. The complex composition of semi-metallic pads, including metallic fibers and non-metallic compounds, can cause uneven heat distribution and localized hotspots. This uneven heating can lead to inconsistent friction where different parts of the pad experience different wear rates or failure modes. The unpredictable element you're observing may be the result of this material variability interacting with the thermal stresses during intense deceleration cycles, leading to erratic pedal feel as the heat could soften or warp the pad, or cause separation from the backing plate at unpredictable points. Ceramic composites, on the other hand, tend to maintain a consistent form, which mitigates erratic behavior as they're generally more stable thermally.",
